{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfcUJwjT5sXjzpiiNnzSs", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This regex matches a date in the `YYYY-MM-DD` form:\n\n```regex\n^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])$\n```\n\n- `\\d{4}` is a four-digit year.\n- `(0[1-9]|1[0-2])` is a month from 01 to 12.\n- `(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])` is a day from 01 to 31.\n- `^` and `$` require the whole string to be the date. Use `\\b` instead if you're searching for dates inside longer text.\n\nIt matches `2026-10-01` and rejects `2026-13-01` and `2026-10-32`. It only checks the format, so it still accepts dates that don't exist, like `2026-02-30`. To catch those, parse the string after it matches, for example with Python's `date.fromisoformat(s)` or by checking that JavaScript's `new Date(s)` is valid. A regex that also handles month lengths and leap years can be written, but it's long and hard to maintain."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 315, "cache_read_input_tokens": 24584, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 315}, "output_tokens": 345, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "7cfe1817-9f0b-44a8-9102-aa89e3875a86", "uuid": "3933966c-a759-4d30-a44d-e228520a86ee", "timestamp": "2026-10-02T00:34:24.236Z", "request_id": "req_011CfcUJwQrYnmB39PikK9Yk"}
